{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>My son, to my words listen, incline your ear to my sayings; let them not depart from your eyes, keep them in the midst of your heart; for they are life to those who find them, and to all his flesh a healing (Proverbs 4:20).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, informs us here (Proverbs 4) that a person should listen to the words of Torah and incline his ear to hear them rather than other things — this is the sense of 'incline your ear.' And so he mentioned in another place (ibid. 2): 'incline your heart to discernment,' admonishing that one should incline his heart and turn his thought away from all other occupations for the purpose of discernment, that is, to contemplate discernment. And he called the commandments 'words,' twenty-four times saying 'sayings' (אמרים), and he warned against forgetting the incidental (שכחת המקרה) and against forgetting the essential (שכחת העיקר). 'Let them not depart from your eyes' — this is forgetting the incidental: he means, let not those occupations and distractions depart from your eyes, and the intent is that we pay attention to them and remember them always, in the manner of (Exodus 13:9): ״ולזכרון בין עיניך״, so that we not forget them on account of any incident that befalls us. 'Keep them in the midst of your heart' — this is forgetting the essential: for a thing hidden within a person's heart, which he constantly guards, he will never forget; so too a person is obligated to keep all the commandments within his heart, so that the trait of forgetfulness does not spread to the essence of any commandment, to the point where he thinks it was never given at all. And he gave a reason why: 'for they are life to those who find them' — for Torah and the commandment are life for the soul and healing for the body. 'For they are life to those who find them' — this is the life of the soul; and so he mentioned above (Proverbs 3:22): ״ויהיו חיים לנפשך וחן לגרגרותיך״, and the meaning of 'to your throat' is: when you fulfill the words that you bring forth from your mouth, then grace rises upon you, in the manner of what they said: 'comely in expounding and comely in fulfilling.' And he said further (ibid. 4): ״נצרה כי היא חייך״. And since our Torah is the fruit of supernal wisdom, as it is written in it (Ecclesiastes 7:12): ״החכמה תחיה בעליה״, therefore he mentioned in it 'for they are life' — the branch is drawn from the root, and the spring from the source called 'a spring of living waters,' as the prophet said in his rebuke (Jeremiah 2:13): ״אותי עזבו מקור מים חיים״, and it is written (ibid. 10): ״הוא אלהים חיים״, and those who cleave to Him are called 'life,' in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״ואתם הדבקים בה' אלהיכם חיים כלכם היום״. And he added to explain, 'to those who find them' — that is, to those who find them they are life, and he comes to hint in this expression that not all find them. And why do they not find them? Because they do not search after them; and if they were to search, they would find, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 2:4–5): ״אם תבקשנה ככסף וכמטמונים תחפשנה אז תבין יראת ה'״ and so forth. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'to those who find them' — 'to those who bring them forth (למוציאיהם) with the mouth.' For besides the obligation that a person know the matters in his heart, he is further obligated that they be fluent in his mouth and that he accustom his tongue to them; and concerning both of these Solomon, peace be upon him, said (ibid. 22): ״כי נעים כי תשמרם בבטנך יכונו יחד על שפתיך״. 'And to all his flesh a healing' — the way of a physician who gives a sick person one kind of drug or sustenance to eat, or one of the drinks to drink, that will benefit that particular organ in which the illness resides and which he is treating, but will harm another organ; thus the remedy is not uniform nor complete for all the organs of the body. Therefore he explained that Torah has a great advantage, in that its healing is uniform throughout all the flesh and beneficial to all the organs alike, without any harm to any one of them. And Solomon teaches you in this verse that there is in the performance of the commandments benefit for both body and soul. And so Moses, peace be upon him, explained to us (Deuteronomy 6): ״לטוב לנו״ — for our good — all the days, that is, the World to Come; ״לחיותנו״ — to keep us alive — as this day, that is, this world. And therefore the commandments of the Torah come, some of them received (מקובלות) and some of them rational (מושכלות): the received ones corresponding to the benefit of the soul, the rational ones corresponding to the benefit of the body. For there is in the performance of the commandments benefit and reward at the end, and this is what David, peace be upon him, said concerning all the commandments (Psalms 19:12): ״גם עבדך נזהר בהם בשמרם עקב רב״, and the expression 'עקב' is from the language of 'עָקֵב' (heel), for the heel is the end of the body, and so too the reward and recompense come at the end, for reward is the end of the deed. And this expression 'עקב' we find both in the received commandments, called 'חקים' (statutes), and in the rational ones, called 'משפטים' (ordinances). In the received ones, called statutes, this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 119): ״נטיתי לבי לעשות חקיך לעולם עקב״, and he further said (ibid.): ״הורני ה' דרך חקיך ואצרנה עקב״, that is, I will treasure that quality, because it has an 'end' (עקב), and this reward is the great good hidden away, in the manner of what is written (ibid. 31): ״מה רב טובך״, and this is the meaning of 'עקב רב.' In the rational commandments, called ordinances, this is what is written:" "<b>And it shall come to pass, because (עקב) you will hear these ordinances and keep them and do them, that HaShem your Elohim will keep for you the covenant and the lovingkindness which He swore to your fathers.</b> All the promises of this parashah are bodily promises, for the benefit of the body in this world, which serve as preparation for the life of the World to Come. And a further benefit is included in the word 'עקב,' for these bodily promises are the light and lowly reward that a person receives, for so the heel is the lowliest part of the body; but the great and mighty reward, which is the radiance of the Shekhinah, is included in the word 'והיה,' which embraces the Ineffable Name. And this is why the preceding passage concluded with ״היום לעשותם״ and was immediately followed by ״והיה עקב״, to hint that through doing them 'this day' his reward will be the radiance of the Shekhinah; but the Torah wished to expound upon what is perceptible and elaborate on it, and to hint at what is intelligible and be brief about it — for such is the way throughout all the Torah. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded on the expression 'עקב' that it is an admonition to a person concerning those commandments that a person tramples under his heels, that he not treat them lightly — for there are people who are not careful about certain commandments that seem trivial in their eyes, and they trample them underfoot. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, already revealed to us that the punishment for the commandments a person belittles and tramples under his heels in this world surrounds him on the Day of Judgment, as it is said (Psalms 49:6): ״עון עקבי יסובני״. And 'עון עקבי יסובני' may further include an admonition concerning those commandments that one is obligated to stride toward — and these are strides of a mitzvah, such as going to the synagogue and to the house of study, and visiting the sick, and accompanying the dead, and comforting mourners — all of these are commandments of strides, and their reward is great. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, already said that even on the Sabbath it is permitted to run to the synagogue, as it is said (Hosea 11:10): ״אחרי ה' ילכו כאריה ישאג״, and if he does not fulfill the commandments of strides, that sin will surround him on the Day of Judgment — this is ״עון עקבי יסובני״. And likewise, if he strides toward a transgression, that sin too will surround him on the Day of Judgment, as we find in the case of the daughter of Rabbi Ḥanina ben Teradyon, who was sentenced to sit in a brothel because she took measured steps before the great ones of Rome; and she justified the judgment upon herself and said (Jeremiah 32:19): ״גדול העצה ורב העליליה אשר עיניך פקוחות על כל דרכי בני אדם״ — that is, even regarding the precision of strides. From this you learn that a person receives reward for his strides according to the path on which he walks — this is ״והיה עקב תשמעון״." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״והיה עקב תשמעון״ — the reward of the righteous is only at the end, but the reward of the wicked is immediate, in this world, as it is said (Deuteronomy 7:10): ״ומשלם לשונאיו אל פניו״ — their reward is before Him; but the righteous — their reward comes after, as it is said (Psalms 94:15): ״ואחריו כל ישרי לב״. Hence you say: ״והיה עקב תשמעון״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:54.156291608Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}